items/thm-euclidean-implicit-function-theorem.md:58:1.1 Define $H:U\to\mathbb R^{m+n}$ by $H(x,y):=(x,F(x,y))$. From [L2], the remainder after the linear map $(u,v)\mapsto(u,DF(x,y)(u,v))$ is $(0,r(u,v))$, so $H$ is differentiable with $$DH(x,y)(u,v)=(u,D_xF(x,y)u+D_yF(x,y)v).$$ Its matrix entries are continuous because those of $DF$ are, so $H$ is $C^1$. If $B:=D_yF(a,b)^{-1}$, the displayed derivative at $(a,b)$ has the two-sided inverse $(r,s)\mapsto(r,B(s-D_xF(a,b)r))$. Thus $DH(a,b)$ is invertible. [L2, L3, given, algebra]
